### P2-2: Optimize Track Interpolation Performance

**Status**: Not Started
**Effort**: Small (2 units)
**Dependencies**: P0-6 (Integration tests with performance baseline)
**Spec Reference**: Root README "Performance: 60fps target" • **Status Reference**: STATUS-2025-12-09-050344.md Section "Success Metrics"

#### Description
Profile and optimize Track.getValue() for high-frequency calls during animation. Ensure 60fps target met even with many simultaneous tracks.

#### Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Performance profiling completed (Chrome DevTools)
- [ ] Bottlenecks identified in interpolation path
- [ ] Optimizations applied (memoization, early returns, etc.)
- [ ] Benchmark shows < 16ms frame time with 50 active tracks
- [ ] No memory leaks in repeated getValue() calls
- [ ] Tests still pass after optimization

#### Technical Notes
Profiling approach:
```typescript
// Benchmark test
const tracks = Array.from({ length: 50 }, () =>
  new Track({ from: 0, to: 100, duration: 1000, easing: 'easeOutQuart' })
);

const startTime = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
  tracks.forEach(track => track.getValue(i));
}
const elapsed = performance.now() - startTime;
console.log(`1000 frames, 50 tracks: ${elapsed}ms (${elapsed/1000}ms per frame)`);
```

Target: < 5ms per frame for interpolation overhead

Optimization candidates:
1. Cache easing function results for common progress values
2. Early return for completed tracks
3. Avoid object creation in hot path
4. Use typed arrays for number[] interpolation

